Why this is good software design

Remember our Design Patterns discussions?

One principle kept appearing:

Single Responsibility Principle

Look at these APIs.

state_dict()

Responsibility:

Extract state.

Nothing else.

torch.save()

Responsibility:

Serialize and store.

Nothing else.

torch.load()

Responsibility:

Deserialize.

Nothing else.

load_state_dict()

Responsibility:

Update an existing model.

Nothing else.

Each API has one reason to change.

That's elegant software design.

Why not save the whole model?

You may wonder:

torch.save(model)

This actually exists.

So why have we spent so much time on state_dict()?

Because saving the whole model couples the saved file to:

the exact Python class,
the module path,
the code layout.

Imagine this.

Today:

models.py

↓

class MyModel

Tomorrow you refactor:

network.py

↓

class MyModel

The saved object may no longer load correctly because it expects the old class location.

With state_dict():

Weights

↓

Portable

As long as you recreate the architecture, the learned state can usually be loaded.

That's why almost all production code, research code, and libraries—including Hugging Face—prefer saving state, not the entire Python object.

Real Engineering Example

Suppose you're training Gemma.

You don't save:

Entire Python process

You save:

Architecture (defined in code)

+

Learned state

+

Optional training checkpoint

The Four APIs
API	Responsibility	Returns
model.state_dict()	Extract persistent model state	OrderedDict
torch.save(obj)	Serialize any supported Python object to disk	Writes a file
torch.load(path)	Read and reconstruct the saved object	Original Python object
model.load_state_dict(state)	Copy saved state into an existing model	Updates the model in place
Key Design Principle

These APIs are separated because they solve different problems:

Extract state (state_dict)
Store object (torch.save)
Recover object (torch.load)
Apply state (load_state_dict)
Biggest takeaway

When you see:

torch.save(model.state_dict(), "model.pt")

Don't think of it as one operation.

Think of it as two independent steps:

Model
   │
   ├── Extract persistent state
   ▼
OrderedDict
   │
   ├── Serialize and write to disk
   ▼
File

That mental separation will make many later concepts—including checkpoints, Hugging Face model packaging, and deployment—feel much more natural.
